# Licensing

This repository is **multi-licensed** per [DR-018](docs/decisions/DR-018.md). Each
artifact's own `LICENSE` file is authoritative; this root file is the pointer and
summary. Copyright (c) 2026 Audit&Fix (sole trader, ABN 19429399114).

| Artifact | License | File |
|---|---|---|
| `packages/shared` | **MPL-2.0** | [`packages/shared/LICENSE`](packages/shared/LICENSE) |
| `packages/minspec` | **MIT** | [`packages/minspec/LICENSE`](packages/minspec/LICENSE) |
| `packages/extension-pack` | **MIT** | [`packages/extension-pack/LICENSE`](packages/extension-pack/LICENSE) |
| `scripts/`, root tooling | **MIT** | this file, MIT text below |
| `docs/`, `sites/` copy, prose | **CC-BY-4.0** | [`LICENSE-CONTENT`](LICENSE-CONTENT) |

**Why the split.** `packages/shared` is the one reusable library, so it carries
MPL-2.0's file-level copyleft: modifications to those source files stay open, while any
larger work - including a commercial one - may still depend on and link it. The
extensions are end-user apps with no library to protect, so they are MIT to maximise
adoption. Prose is content, so it takes a content licence rather than a code one.

**Combining MPL and MIT is not a conflict.** MPL-2.0 is file-level copyleft: the MIT
works may combine with the MPL files freely, and only the MPL'd source files themselves
carry the share-modifications obligation. This is the standard "MPL library inside a
permissive app" arrangement - no relicensing of the extensions, and no obligation on
consumers beyond the shared files they modify.

